  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Resource Development / Establish Accounts — Cloud Accounts", "technique": "T1585.003", "what_happened": "At 2026-05-13 10:02:11 CEST, Wazuh's Office365/Azure module logged a burst of OAuth authorize-endpoint probes. data.srcip=45.143.166.92 issued GET /oauth2/authorize requests carrying client_id values for four never-before-seen applications whose display names deliberately mimic legitimate corporate productivity tools — 'Corp_HR_Helper_official', 'Corp_IT_Assistant_v2', 'Corp_Mail_Smart_Search', 'Corp_Finance_DocViewer'. Every request requested the broad scope set 'Mail.Read Files.Read.All offline_access' and carried a tenant context header for a brand-new external tenant ('trustedvendor-collab', tenant_age 12 days). Wazuh fired custom rule 91310 ('OAuth consent from tenant_age < 30 days') and rule 91220 ('OAuth app name mimics legitimate corp service'). The lookalike naming is the establish-accounts signature: a freshly created tenant registering apps whose names impersonate the victim org's own IT/HR/Finance tooling to lower suspicion at the consent prompt.", "chain_reason": "rule.groups contained 'office365' and rule.description matched 'OAuth' / 'consent' / 'tenant age' with data.srcip=45.143.166.92 against data.dstip=10.108.57.184. The univariate signature: a single external source driving GET /oauth2/authorize for multiple unknown client_id values, all bearing corp-lookalike display names, from a tenant younger than 30 days, inside a 3-minute window.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T10:02:11+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Credential Access / Steal Application Access Token — escalating scope", "technique": "T1528", "what_happened": "From 10:05:30 to 10:11:20 CEST, the same data.srcip=45.143.166.92 issued a sequence of POST /oauth2/token requests against the IdP host 10.108.57.184 using grant_type=client_credentials for client_id='Corp_Finance_DocViewer', with the requested scope ESCALATING on each call: first single scopes ('Mail.Read', then 'Files.Read.All', then 'Calendars.Read', then 'User.Read.All'), then a combined 'Mail.Read Files.Read.All offline_access', and finally the maximal set 'Mail.Read Files.Read.All User.Read.All Calendars.Read offline_access'. Wazuh fired rule 91220 again and the token endpoint rules. The escalating-scope pattern is a silent-consent probe: the attacker is testing which permission grants the corp tenant will issue to the lookalike app before committing.", "chain_reason": "data.srcip=45.143.166.92 POSTed repeatedly to the /oauth2/token endpoint on data.dstip=10.108.57.184 with grant_type=client_credentials for the SAME client_id seen bearing a corp-lookalike name in step 1, with monotonically widening scope strings. rule.description matched 'OAuth token' / 'consent' and the office365 rule group fired. The offline_access scope in the final two requests is the persistence marker.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T10:05:30+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Collection — external tenant access to Finance/HR resources", "technique": "T1213.002", "what_happened": "From 10:12:40 to 10:15:55 CEST, data.srcip=45.143.166.92 presented a bearer token from the new tenant and issued GET /api/sharepoint requests against 10.108.57.184 targeting sensitive corporate resource paths: /Finance/Invoices, /Finance/BankingDetails, /HR/PayrollQ3, /HR/EmployeeData. Each request carried the external-tenant header for 'trustedvendor-collab' (tenant_age 12 days). Wazuh fired custom rule 91312 ('External tenant accessing Finance/HR resources'). This is the post-establishment access stage: the lookalike-named app from the freshly created tenant is now reaching for Finance and HR document stores.", "chain_reason": "data.srcip=45.143.166.92 generated a sequence of /api/sharepoint/Finance/* and /api/sharepoint/HR/* requests on data.dstip=10.108.57.184 immediately after the token probing in step 2, with rule.id 91312 firing on external-tenant access to resources tagged Finance/HR. The combination new-tenant + sensitive-resource is the canonical establish-accounts cloud-access signature from Playbook 17.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T10:12:40+0200"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Resource Development / Establish Accounts — Cloud Accounts (app registration)", "technique": "T1585.003", "what_happened": "From 10:17:05 to 10:18:47 CEST, data.srcip=45.143.166.92 issued POST /api/app-registrations requests from the 'trustedvendor-collab' tenant, registering two new applications whose display names impersonate corporate services: 'Corp IT Assistant' (description 'Official Corp IT Helper', reply URL https://corp-it-helper.trustedvendor-collab.com/cb) and 'Corp Finance Portal' (description 'Corp Finance Document Access', reply URL https://corp-finance.trustedvendor-collab.io/auth). Wazuh fired rule 91220 ('OAuth app name mimics legitimate corp service'). The attacker is establishing durable, clean-looking app identities under their own tenant — lookalike names plus attacker-controlled reply URLs — for use in follow-on consent-phishing against corp employees.", "chain_reason": "data.srcip=45.143.166.92 POSTed to /api/app-registrations on data.dstip=10.108.57.184 with displayName values impersonating corp IT/Finance services and reply URLs on the attacker's own trustedvendor-collab.* domains. rule.id 91220 fired on the corp-lookalike app name. App registration under the new tenant is the closing establish-accounts move — it creates the reusable malicious identity asset.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T10:17:05+0200"}
  ],
